and himself around his shoulders he set the four-layered shield,
and on his mighty head the well-made helmet he placed,
horse-tailed, and fearsomely the crest above nodded;

And then Odysseus’ knees were loosed, and his dear heart,
as he saw them putting the armour about themselves, and in their hands spears
long brandishing;

“O father, I myself in this erred—and no one else
is to blame—I who the close-fitted door of the chamber
left leaning open;

“Zeus-sprung son of Laertes, many-devising Odysseus,
and that ruinous man again, whom we ourselves suspect,
is going to the chamber;

and together feet and hands they bound with a heart-grieving bond,
very well twisting them back, right through, as ordered
the son of Laertes, much-enduring, heavenly Odysseus;
